<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">Viewer (mobile / PC device)</data>
      <data key="d6">UserConsumerWebMobile</data>
      <data key="d7">End viewer who opens a link to a personalized video (e.g. a monthly telco bill) from a mobile or PC device. The player requests video segments listed in the playlist file.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">API Gateway</data>
      <data key="d6">ApiGateway</data>
      <data key="d7">First stop of a rendering request into the RaaS ecosystem. Usage plans and API keys are configured per customer to control the load on the rendering farm.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">Lambda - RaaS service frontend (validate rendering requests)</data>
      <data key="d6">Lambda</data>
      <data key="d7">RaaS service frontend. Validates the rendering request, pushes rendering instructions to an SQS queue, and in the same invocation pulls the quick response from the response SQS queue, returning a link to a playlist file.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">SQS #1 - rendering jobs queue</data>
      <data key="d6">SQS</data>
      <data key="d7">Holds rendering instructions. Engine services pull rendering jobs from it when they have capacity.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">EC2 Auto Scaling group - rendering engines (spot)</data>
      <data key="d6">EC2</data>
      <data key="d7">Video rendering engines running entirely on spot instances in an Auto Scaling group. Instances are spread across multiple Availability Zones with diversified instance types for high availability. They push CloudWatch metrics used for scaling. On a spot interruption the instance stops pulling from the queue, drains current jobs, and is detached from the Auto Scaling group. Planned next step: use Auto Scaling group instance weights to represent rendering capacity.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">SQS #2 - response queue</data>
      <data key="d6">SQS</data>
      <data key="d7">The rendering engine responds immediately to this queue so the response is as quick as possible. The same Lambda function invocation pulls from it.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6">
      <data key="d5">S3 - video segments</data>
      <data key="d6">S3</data>
      <data key="d7">Stores the video segments uploaded by the rendering engine. Acts as the origin for CloudFront.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7">
      <data key="d5">CloudFront</data>
      <data key="d6">CloudFront</data>
      <data key="d7">Serves the video segments to the player, pulling them from the S3 origin.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8">
      <data key="d5">CloudWatch (events and metrics)</data>
      <data key="d6">CloudWatch</data>
      <data key="d7">CloudWatch Events notify a Lambda function that a specific spot instance ID is about to be taken (two-minute interruption notice). CloudWatch metrics pushed by the rendering instances feed the scaling mechanism.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="9">
      <data key="d5">Lambda - spot interruption handler</data>
      <data key="d6">Lambda</data>
      <data key="d7">Reacts to spot instance termination events. Calls an API on the specific instance telling it to stop pulling rendering requests from the queue and drain the jobs it is already rendering. Once that call finishes, the instance is detached from the Auto Scaling group. Jobs are expected to finish within the two-minute window.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="10">
      <data key="d5">Step Functions</data>
      <data key="d6">StepFunctions</data>
      <data key="d7">Triggers the scaling Lambda function every 15 seconds.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="11">
      <data key="d5">Lambda - scaling function</data>
      <data key="d6">Lambda</data>
      <data key="d7">Consumes CloudWatch metrics pushed from the rendering instances. When more traffic is expected or capacity is insufficient, it tells the Auto Scaling group to increase the number of in-service instances (scale up/down).</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The viewer opens the link to the personalized video; the rendering request arrives at API Gateway.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="0" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">The player on the mobile or PC device requests a video segment through the CloudFront link.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">API Gateway forwards the rendering request to the RaaS frontend Lambda function.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">After validating the request, the Lambda pushes rendering instructions to the SQS queue.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="0" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The Lambda returns a link to a playlist file that contains links to the video segments.</data>
      <data key="d10">6</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Rendering engines pull rendering jobs from the queue when they have capacity.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The rendering engine immediately responds to a response SQS queue for the quickest possible response.</data>
      <data key="d10">4</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The rendering engine uploads the video segments to S3.</data>
      <data key="d10">7</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="5" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">The same Lambda function invocation pulls the response from the queue.</data>
      <data key="d10">5</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9" />
      <data key="d10">8</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6" target="7" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">S3 returns the video segment to CloudFront.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="7" target="6" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">CloudFront requests the video segment from the S3 origin.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="7" target="0" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">CloudFront delivers the video segment, which is played to the user.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="8" target="9" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">A CloudWatch event notifies the Lambda function that a specific spot instance ID is about to be taken (two-minute interruption notice).</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="8" target="11" id="0">
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">CloudWatch metrics are consumed by the scaling Lambda function.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">meta</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="9" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">The Lambda calls an API on the specific instance telling it to stop pulling rendering requests and drain its current jobs; the instance is then detached from the Auto Scaling group.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="10" target="11" id="0">
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">Step Functions triggers the scaling Lambda function every 15 seconds.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="11" target="8" id="0">
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">The scaling Lambda queries the CloudWatch metrics pushed from the rendering instances.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">meta</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="11" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">The Lambda tells the Auto Scaling group to increase (or decrease) the number of in-service instances based on expected traffic.</data>
      <data key="d10">3</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">SundaySky Rendering as a Service (RaaS) on EC2 Spot Instances</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6CgqEzyWpeA</data>
    <data key="d2">SundaySky's rendering-as-a-service ecosystem for personalized videos. Requests enter through API Gateway and a Lambda frontend, are queued in SQS, and are rendered by an EC2 Auto Scaling group running entirely on spot instances. Video segments are uploaded to S3 and served through CloudFront. High availability comes from spreading instances across multiple Availability Zones and diversifying instance types. Spot interruptions (two-minute notice) are handled by a CloudWatch event and a Lambda that drains the instance. A custom scaling mechanism uses CloudWatch metrics and a Lambda triggered by Step Functions every 15 seconds. Scale: billions of videos yearly, millions daily.</data>
    <data key="d3">Media, Video Rendering, Serverless, Spot Instances, Auto Scaling</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
